#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055844} :activity/id #uuid "0e31a49b-f54b-50cd-ab36-02966e00c737" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/math-academy-id 35} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055962 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x)$, we can express the derivative as $f'(x)$.\n\nRecall that we can also express the derivative using the **differential operator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the differential operator while computing the derivative, too. For example, the power rule can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(x^n \\right) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\big(\\cdots\\big)\n$$\n\njust means that we need to differentiate whatever is inside $\\big(\\cdots\\big)$ with respect to $x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "537fb074-19a0-542c-9a84-c4542e26b33f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2647 :tutorial/title "The Differential Operator"} :step/id #uuid "219dccb2-c3f8-5ef8-af33-9ea8f7e5c0f8" :step/math-academy-id 12596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055978 :tutorial/content "We know how to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nusing the definition of the derivative. However, there is a trick to do it faster!\n\nThe trick is called the **power rule**, and it states that for a general power function in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthe derivative is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nThe function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\ntakes the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^n\n$$\n\nwith $n=2$, so we can use the power rule to compute its derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= nx^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{1} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result agrees with what we would get if we used the definition of the derivative. However, using the power rule, the computations are much easier!" :tutorial/id #uuid "37492dee-669b-5eb2-9676-b78afaf6acb4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 337 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ac48d370-ba29-5343-bf37-4b82f3611773" :step/math-academy-id 625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054455 :question/id #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055196}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "ea42d926-8045-5d22-9aa7-2fd3a3f233c8" :step/math-academy-id 626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054456 :question/id #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" :question/math-academy-id "e-135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055347}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "8893f4d9-c611-591e-80a2-799835854aae" :step/math-academy-id 629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054457 :question/id #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" :question/math-academy-id "e-136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050894}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519521}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "41c0eff0-f259-5568-b570-bafddf204af8" :step/math-academy-id 631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055983 :tutorial/content "In general, if\n\n$$\nf(x) = k\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant, then $f'(x) = 0$. There are a couple ways to see why this is.\n\nFirst, let's take the constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\n$$\n\nbut write it a little differently:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2x^0\n$$\n\nTo differentiate the function above, we can use the power rule with $n=0$. Applying the power rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 0 \\cdot 2x^{0 - 1} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2x^{-1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere's nothing special about the number $2$. The same result would hold for *any* constant function.\n\nWe can also think about this geometrically. The function $y=f(x)=2$ is a horizontal line, and it does not rise or fall as we change $x$. So, its slope is zero everywhere, and therefore $f'(x) = 0$ everywhere.\n\n![](images/22/222febb3babc289ba15ba1e187aa9848ea6f2d802bf21ad8e1396b092370d564.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "aba9943d-6c1b-5f0d-b9e0-a0c854c231b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 341 :tutorial/title "The Derivative of the Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "421f20ea-c000-57af-973a-3496371dd497" :step/math-academy-id 633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054465 :question/id #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "654f8868-880b-53dc-ba4c-677aecb10b95" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048262}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519576}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Constant Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "814ed39b-61cd-5eb5-92ed-72ff4dbe9a1b" :step/math-academy-id 634}] :activity/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054897}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519665} :activity/id #uuid "8d6616d8-4bd9-5180-836d-7a649de8f37f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/math-academy-id 35} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38afd3e0-5bd1-5aff-bde9-5ab30100b8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519078} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb3a68d-3bc9-5a2e-aa01-8fd19f73ef1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{-100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519114} {:answer/id #uuid "982e4a51-8007-577a-887e-87a9e653b741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519242} {:answer/id #uuid "d47195f6-f6ac-5acf-9ac4-5fd2c4c5e7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519340} {:answer/id #uuid "eea7b9dd-78cc-5e09-a8b2-9e91f80d4798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519384}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519340} :answer-field/id #uuid "6151e229-0e35-564f-8d7f-5b331c2a89a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7712c3e1-7e39-5f21-96a2-73fbade1bb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-47827" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-100.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "64201a69-4087-5fa9-8613-459e7ef99140" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f3bef3-f4f9-50bd-ad1b-980bacc7743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519030} {:answer/id #uuid "1a413cde-51aa-554a-a668-cb72400d7998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519032} {:answer/id #uuid "22ad66f0-9dd6-500b-bfa1-89777d6d096e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519046} {:answer/id #uuid "93027069-aad5-57c6-9c9d-a24023cbdefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519234} {:answer/id #uuid "b42a8058-7cc4-5b42-8aee-1b65e58718bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519030} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2dfe4a5-5448-5650-8cd1-67d2d7210c41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "adc818d6-3be7-557c-93b2-e7ce26f749ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-102182" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{1}{e}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "bcda3cd1-5fdc-5593-8afc-4d6c3c5925cb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3490041-c6e1-534b-ae84-df116add6226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519262} {:answer/id #uuid "da522b4a-46a4-5729-803b-4a8ed45b4b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519352} {:answer/id #uuid "f16e8de5-d93f-5c40-b48d-326156982a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519389} {:answer/id #uuid "f927c200-f0bd-5da8-96d4-fd1b9ab95850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519406} {:answer/id #uuid "fcac43f6-9d84-52e6-bbc3-c7111b1c3113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519409} :answer-field/id #uuid "db460c81-b20d-5e16-92de-4539fa40eda4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0cf33d77-f213-525d-99fb-8b9182428af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-12244" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ when $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/2}}={x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2\\,-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d2cd4ad0-adea-528f-a78f-3a888cbb65e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519637}}] :activity/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518981}]}
